export default "Perimenopause can feel messy. One month may look normal. The next may bring a short cycle, poor sleep, and hot flashes.\n\nThis tracker groups symptoms so a long list feels manageable. It cannot diagnose perimenopause, rule out other causes, or tell you what treatment to use.\n\n## There is no official symptom count\n\nYou may see lists of 34, 66, or 100 perimenopause symptoms online. Those counts are not official medical checklists. Major medical sources group symptoms instead, focusing on period changes, hot flashes, sleep, mood, vaginal and urinary symptoms, sex pain, and body changes. Your list does not need to match anyone else's. Track what is new, repeated, or hard on daily life.\n\n## Start with urgent concerns\n\nDo this section first, before anything else.\n\n| Concern | Happened? | What I will do |\n|---|---|---|\n| I feel unsafe or may hurt myself | | |\n| Bleeding after 12 months with no period or spotting | | |\n| Very heavy bleeding or bleeding that worries me | | |\n| Severe pelvic pain | | |\n| Chest pain, fainting, or severe shortness of breath | | |\n\nIf something feels urgent, get care now. Do not wait to finish a tracker. If danger is immediate, use emergency services. If you are in suicidal crisis or emotional distress and not in immediate physical danger, contact 988 in the United States.\n\n## Your baseline\n\nFill this out before you start tracking day to day.\n\n| Question | Your answer |\n|---|---|\n| Usual cycle length before changes started | |\n| Usual period length | |\n| Usual heavy days | |\n| Date changes began | |\n| Current age | |\n| Current birth control or hormone use | |\n| Medicines or supplements | |\n| Fibroids, polyps, PCOS, endometriosis, or thyroid history | |\n\nBring this baseline to a visit. It helps your clinician judge what is new for you.\n\n## Daily symptom log\n\nUse one row per day. Fill in what matters; leave the rest blank. Rating key: 0 = none, 1 = mild, 2 = clear, 3 = strong or disruptive.\n\n| Date | Bleeding | Hot flashes | Night sweats | Sleep | Mood | Headache | Brain fog | Vaginal dryness | Daily impact |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| | None / spotting / flow | 0 / 1 / 2 / 3 | 0 / 1 / 2 / 3 | Good / broken / poor | Steady / anxious / low / irritable | No / mild / strong | No / mild / strong | No / mild / strong | None / some / big |\n| | | | | | | | | | |\n| | | | | | | | | | |\n\n## Bleeding pattern log\n\nPerimenopause can make bleeding less predictable. Track cycle gaps, heavy days, clots, and spotting separately if bleeding is your main concern.\n\n| Date | Cycle day, if known | Flow | Clots | Pain 0 to 10 | Spotting after sex | New for you? |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| | | Spotting / light / medium / heavy | None / small / large | | Yes / no | Yes / no |\n| | | | | | | |\n\nCycle gap tracker for when periods skip or come close together:\n\n| Bleed start date | Bleed end date | Days since last bleed started | Total bleed days | Heaviest day | New for you? |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| | | | | | Yes / no |\n| | | | | | Yes / no |\n\nContact a clinician about bleeding that is much heavier than usual, lasts longer than 7 days, happens between periods and is new for you, happens after sex, comes with large new clots, or happens after 12 months with no period. Seek urgent care if bleeding is heavy enough that you feel faint, weak, confused, or short of breath.\n\n## Hot flash and night sweat details\n\n| Date | Time | What happened | What was happening before it | What helped |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| | | Heat / sweat / chills / racing heart | Sleep / stress / food / alcohol / unknown | |\n| | | | | |\n\nDo not force a trigger. \"Unknown\" is a useful answer.\n\n## Sleep, mood, and brain fog\n\n- [ ] Hard to fall asleep.\n- [ ] Woke often or too early.\n- [ ] Night sweats woke you.\n- [ ] More anxious, irritable, or low mood.\n- [ ] Tearful, or anger felt harder to control.\n- [ ] Lost words more often, or forgot tasks or names.\n- [ ] Had trouble focusing.\n- [ ] Symptoms affected work, school, care, or relationships.\n\nIf danger is immediate, use emergency services now. If you are in suicidal crisis or emotional distress and not in immediate physical danger, contact 988 in the United States. Privacy can wait.\n\n## Vaginal, urinary, and body changes\n\nSkip this section if you do not want to track it.\n\n- [ ] Vaginal dryness or burning.\n- [ ] Pain with sex.\n- [ ] Bleeding after sex.\n- [ ] More urinary urgency or nighttime bathroom trips.\n- [ ] Headaches, joint aches, or muscle aches changed.\n- [ ] Weight, skin, or hair changed in a way you want to discuss.\n\nYou can keep sex or vaginal notes outside an app if that feels safer.\n\n## Medicine and supplement log\n\nTrack changes only. Do not use this table to change a dose on your own.\n\n| Date | Name | Started / stopped / changed | Dose, if known | What changed |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| | | | | |\n\n## Weekly pattern check\n\n| Question | Your note |\n|---|---|\n| How many days had hot flashes? | |\n| How many nights had sweats or poor sleep? | |\n| Which mood showed up most? | |\n| Did bleeding happen, and was it new for you? | |\n| Did headaches or brain fog cluster? | |\n| What do you want to ask care? | |\n\n## Pick your top three\n\nYou do not need to bring everything to a visit at once.\n\n| Rank | Symptom or concern | Why it matters |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | | |\n| 2 | | |\n| 3 | | |\n\n```text\nMy top concerns are ________, ________, and ________.\nThey affect my life by ________.\nI want help deciding what to track and what care options to discuss.\n```\n\n## Questions for a clinician\n\n- Could these symptoms fit perimenopause? What else should we rule out?\n- Which bleeding changes need follow-up?\n- Could my medicines or hormones affect this pattern?\n- What can I do for hot flashes, night sweats, or sleep?\n- What can help vaginal dryness or sex pain?\n- What symptoms mean I should call sooner?\n- How long should I keep tracking?\n\n## A note on postpartum cycle return\n\nIf you are tracking cycle return after childbirth rather than perimenopause, the patterns and timelines are different. Ovulation can return before your first postpartum period, so an absent period does not mean you cannot conceive; exclusive breastfeeding can delay period return for 6 months or longer through lactational amenorrhea, while combination feeding or formula feeding often sees earlier return, typically between 6 weeks and 6 months. Postpartum lochia (bleeding from the healing uterine wound) is not a period and is not hormonally driven; it transitions from red to pink to white or yellow over about 4 to 6 weeks and should have a gap before true period bleeding begins. If heavy red bleeding returns after lochia has already lightened, or you soak a pad in under an hour, contact your provider. This kit's daily and weekly log format above can be adapted for postpartum tracking, but the reference ranges and red flags differ from perimenopause. Ask your OB or midwife which pattern applies to you.\n\n## Privacy note\n\nPerimenopause notes can include sex, mood, medicine, bleeding, and location details.\n\nBring a short list to care, not every detail: the date your pattern changed, your last few bleeding dates, symptoms that repeat, symptoms that disrupt life, medicine or hormone changes, and your main question.\n\nFloriva can help with private on-device tracking. Use short labels like \"night sweat,\" \"poor sleep,\" or \"heavy flow.\" The goal is a pattern you can read later, not a diagnosis from the app. For visit prep, read [Floriva for gynecologist prep](/app-guides/floriva-for-gynecologist-prep). If bleeding timing itself is your main question, use the [late and missed period log](/free/late-missed-period-log).";
